Fan review complete and saved to `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Freddie-Mercury/2026-08-28_020004/fan.md` (SHA verified against context.json before review).

**Verdict: revise** — trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit. **Blockers: 2, concerns: 5.**

The two blockers are canonical errors an informed fan would correct in the comments: the FAQ asserts Mary Austin "was holding his hand when he died" (no source supports it — the record places Jim Hutton and reportedly Dave Clark at the bedside with Mary phoned after, and the claim lands squarely on the fandom's Mary-vs-Jim fault line), and the Bohemian Rhapsody passage says the opera section's "jury" was "overdubbed from his own voice" when the choir was famously Mercury, May, and Taylor across ~180 overdubs, with Taylor singing the "Galileo" falsetto. Both are one-clause repairs.

**Strongest hit:** the Live Aid inversion — connecting the Wigg "untouchable" fantasy quote to the Wembley set so that "the most extroverted act in rock history was, from the inside, a shy man's safest possible intimacy." That's the sentence a fan shares.

**Highest-priority concern beyond the blockers:** the Munich section's sourcing cluster — the "Every day is Fasching" quote is attributed to Wigg but absent from the on-file transcript, and "returned or pawned" embellishes what the transcript supports (the gifts simply came back — which is the more devastating detail anyway).
